WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.515
Error handling should help operators understand failures without exposing internals to

00:00:04.515 --> 00:00:05.285
users.

00:00:05.285 --> 00:00:09.735
Production responses should avoid stack traces, internal file paths, dependency versions,

00:00:09.735 --> 00:00:11.945
database messages, and implementation details.

00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:16.140
A user can receive a clear error category and request identifier while the detailed

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.520
diagnostic information remains in protected operational systems.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.180
Structured logging helps teams search, alert, and investigate behavior across services.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:28.420
Useful fields may include request IDs, user or tenant identifiers where appropriate,

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:32.520
route names, status codes, latency, dependency failures, and security-relevant events.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.360
The logging design should avoid secrets, session tokens, authorization headers,

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.510
password values, raw sensitive payloads,

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:41.755
and personally sensitive data that is not needed for operations.

00:00:41.755 --> 00:00:44.630
Observability is also part of incident response readiness.

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:49.030
A Node.js service should make it possible to notice unusual authentication failures,

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:53.470
authorization denials, validation failures, dependency warnings, rate-limit events,

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:56.415
unexpected outbound requests, and runtime errors.

00:00:56.415 --> 00:01:00.150
Dependency and runtime warnings should not be ignored simply because the service appears

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.990
to work. They may signal security or reliability drift.

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:06.560
The balance is to understand behavior without turning logs into a data leak.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:11.120
Logs often travel to third-party platforms, shared dashboards, long-term storage, backups,

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.440
or developer laptops. Treat logs as sensitive operational data.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:18.660
Good observability helps teams detect and respond to problems while preserving privacy,

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:22.600
protecting secrets, and keeping production details out of untrusted responses.
